  :doc/title "Co-Scientist iteration 10 - apply-quad is flat in both db size and value shape, so the fold's 27 unaccounted CPU-seconds are still unattributed"
  :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-10.edn"
  :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 10 - apply-quad eliminated, and the 27 seconds is still unattributed

> Iteration 09 found the read's cost. This one was supposed to find the fold's,
> and instead it removes the leading candidate. Recording an elimination is the
> point: six inferences about where this time goes have now been wrong, and the
> only thing that has ever settled one is a measurement.

## S. What the phase probe narrowed it to

`control-plane` b38afdf8 split the fold's phases by which store fn each one
calls -- hydrate and novelty read use `async-get-fn` + `decrypt-fn`, the
`qs/commit!` tree rebuild uses `encrypt-fn` + `blind-fn`. On the real graph:

    {\\\"op\\\":\\\"write\\\",\\\"gets\\\":135,\\\"decrypts\\\":1665,\\\"encrypts\\\":0,\\\"blinds\\\":0,\\\"ms\\\":5512}
    outcome=exceededCpu  cpu=32500ms

`encrypts:0` and `blinds:0`: the fold never reaches `qs/commit!` at all. Every
store call finishes by 5.5s. **Roughly 27 seconds is spent making no store
calls whatsoever**, before the rebuild starts.

The only thing `fold!`'s cljs branch runs in that window is

    db (reduce (fn [db q] (apply-quad db q ref?)) hydrated-db novelty-quads)

so `apply-quad` was the obvious suspect: ~2500 quads, ~27s, about 10ms each.

## T. It is not apply-quad, measured twice

`bench/assert_quad_shapes.cljk`. Swept by db size, then by VALUE SHAPE --
because root ADR-2609051700's lesson is that a foundation library's constant
lives in the codec, so a sweep that varies only COUNT can miss it entirely.

    by db size (500 asserts into a db of...)
      base=0     0.0320 ms/quad     base=2000   0.0060 ms/quad
      base=500   0.0080 ms/quad     base=4000   0.0060 ms/quad

    by value shape (n=2500)
      short strings   0.0088 ms/quad     40x repeated string   0.0088 ms/quad
      EDN-encoded map 0.0072 ms/quad     IPLD Link             0.0100-0.0120 ms/quad

**Flat, and flat in db size.** 2500 quads costs 22-30ms, not 27 seconds --
three orders of magnitude apart. `assert-quad` does not grow with the db it
inserts into, and Link values (which route into a fourth index through
`ref?`) cost the same as strings.

## What is left, and what is already excluded

Excluded by measurement, on this problem, in order: I/O (a 24s read reported
`gets:0`), `with-blocks` restarts (handlerRuns under 100 everywhere), AES-GCM
(1800 noble decrypts benchmark 92ms-2.3s), the snapshot half (iteration 09:
already O(result)), the CPU budget (300000 ms is not enough either),
`qs/commit!` (encrypts:0 -- never reached), `blind-fn` (blinds:0 -- never
called), and now `apply-quad`.

**The 27 seconds is unattributed.** What remains inside that window and has
NOT been measured: `hydrate-db-cached`'s own row assembly after
`cold-datoms-async` returns, its `v->edn`/`edn->link` re-encode before
`cache-put!` (which would run BEFORE `encrypt-fn` and so is consistent with
`encrypts:0`), `pmap-async`'s fan-in over the novelty tx blocks, and
`decode-quad-payload`.

## Seeds for iteration 11

  - **Stop narrowing by reading the code.** Six attempts, six misses, every one
    resolved in minutes by a measurement. The window needs timers inside
    `kotobase-peer`, not another argument about what is in it.
  - When those timers exist, run them against a LOCAL reproduction at the real
    shape (1665 rows, 135 blocks) before touching production -- iteration 09
    showed a local repro of this engine is faithful enough to settle a
    structural question.
  - ⚠ Local controls here have used identity crypto. That substitution is what
    produced the unexplained 10x in ADR-2609100100's earlier local comparison.
    Whatever the timers show locally, check it against production before
    believing it."}]
